<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sipahi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The sipahis were cavalrymen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> who held land grants called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timar">timars</a>, from which they collected revenue in return for military service. They formed a core of the Ottoman army in its classical period. Accounts of the Ottoman Empire describe their role in the Ottoman socioeconomic system.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sipahi.jpg" alt="Sipahi" width="311" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dal Mehmed Çelebi (Asafi) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Secaatname_0179_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The sipahi were the professional Turkish heavy cavalry of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. They were taken over from the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum and developed into a standing cavalry force at the end of the 14th century. There were two kinds. The provincial tımarlı sipahi held land grants called tımars and made up most of the Ottoman army, while the kapıkulu sipahi were salaried household troops, serving beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>. The irregular light cavalry known as akıncı, or raiders, were not counted as sipahi. The tımar system was standardised in the second half of the 15th century and had its origin in the Byzantine pronoia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A tımar included arable land that the state owned and that the sipahi supervised in return for collecting taxes, keeping order and serving in the army. The sipahi received no salary. Peasants had the right to farm the land and had to work the sipahi's own plot, the çift, for a number of days. Tımars were small units; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziamet">ziamet</a> was a larger holding for officers, and a has the largest, held by the highest-ranking commanders. A sipahi was bound to join campaigns when the sultan called, bringing a number of armed retainers, the cebelu, according to his revenue. Failure to appear meant losing the tımar, while distinction in battle could enlarge it.</p><p>In war the sipahis and their retainers gathered under their regimental beys, who gathered under the sanjakbeys and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylerbey">beylerbeys</a>. Both flanks of the classical Ottoman army consisted of tımarlı cavalry, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissary</a> infantry and artillery in the centre. The cavalry were the mobile striking arm, opening with skirmishes, charging weaker or isolated units and withdrawing when faced with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, and drawing enemy horse away from its infantry. A sipahi carried a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/composite-bow">composite bow</a>, a sabre, a spear and a shield. The kapıkulu cavalry had six divisions, among them the Sipahis, the Silahdars and the Ulufecis, all paid quarterly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The kapıkulu sipahis considered themselves superior to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>, who were officially slaves recruited through the devşirme, and a Turkish proverb says that horsemen do not mutiny. From the middle of the 16th century the Janissaries gained importance, but the sipahis remained a factor in the empire's economy and politics; in the early years of Sultan Murad IV they were, with the Janissaries, the de facto rulers. In 1826 they helped Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a> disband the Janissaries after a revolt, and two years later he revoked their privileges too. Older sipahis kept their tımar lands for life, and younger ones joined the new army as cavalry. The French army later adopted the term Spahi for cavalry units in its colonial armies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sipahi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The timar system, the empire’s basic agrarian-military institution, assigned revenues from specific agricultural lands to <b>sipahi</b> cavalrymen in exchange for military service, so that land tenure, taxation and military organisation were joined in a single mechanism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The provincial cavalry, the timariot <b>sipahis</b>, made up the bulk of the classical Ottoman army.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
